    <!-- Section 3: How the 50/30/20 Rule Works -->
    <article class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]" data-mrb-t="home.ed_s3_title">
        How the 50/30/20 Rule Works
      </h2>
      <div class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert text-base leading-relaxed text-[#334155] dark:text-[#cbd5e1] space-y-3">
        <p data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s3_p1">
          If you want to know <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">what is the 50/30/20 rule</strong>, it is an intuitive proportional framework that splits take-home pay into three clear target categories:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
          <li data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s3_li1">
            <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">50% Needs:</strong> Essential living expenses required for survival and work—housing, groceries, utilities, basic transportation, and minimum loan payments.
          </li>
          <li data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s3_li2">
            <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">30% Wants:</strong> Non-essential lifestyle expenses like dining out, hobbies, subscriptions, travel, and entertainment.
          </li>
          <li data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s3_li3">
            <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">20% Savings & Debt Repayment:</strong> Future stability funds, including emergency savings, retirement accounts, and extra debt principal reduction.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s3_p2">
          The <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">50/30/20 rule</strong> (also called the <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">50 30 20 rule</strong> or <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">50 30 20 budget rule</strong>) provides a broad benchmark rather than an inflexible mandate. Calculate your target amounts instantly in our <a href="/50-30-20-budget/" class="text-[#0e6e55] dark:text-[#10b981] font-medium underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-80">50/30/20 budget calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 4: What Is a Zero-Based Budget? -->
    <article class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]" data-mrb-t="home.ed_s4_title">
        What Is a Zero-Based Budget?
      </h2>
      <div class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert text-base leading-relaxed text-[#334155] dark:text-[#cbd5e1] space-y-3">
        <p data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s4_p1">
          If you are researching <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">what is a 0 based budget</strong>, it is a method where every single dollar of monthly income is assigned a clear job until none remains unallocated:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3.5 rounded-[10px] bg-white dark:bg-[#111827] border border-[#e5e0d8] dark:border-[#1e293b] font-mono text-sm text-center font-semibold text-[#0e6e55] dark:text-[#10b981] not-prose shadow-whisper">
          Income − Planned Expenses − Savings − Debt Payments = $0
        </div>
        <p data-mrb-t-html="home.ed_s4_p2">
          A <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">zero based budget</strong> (or <strong class="text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]">0 based budget</strong>) does not mean spending down your bank account to zero. Rather, it means giving every dollar a defined destination—bills, groceries, emergency savings, or debt payoff. By preventing unallocated funds from sitting idly, you stop accidental overspending and stay in control. Test this workflow in our <a href="/zero-based-budget/" class="text-[#0e6e55] dark:text-[#10b981] font-medium underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-80">zero-based budget tool</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 5: Monthly and Weekly Budget Planning -->
    <article class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]" data-mrb-t="home.ed_s5_title">
        Budgeting Frequency Normalization Explained
      </h2>
      <div class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert text-base leading-relaxed text-[#334155] dark:text-[#cbd5e1] space-y-3">
        <p data-mrb-t="home.ed_s5_p1">
          Real cash flows rarely match monthly cycles. You might receive weekly paychecks or pay semi-annual insurance. Standard budgeting tools often produce inaccurate totals by assuming a month is four weeks (28 days instead of 30.4 days).
        </p>
        <p data-mrb-t="home.ed_s5_p2">
          Our engine performs annualized frequency conversion: weekly items are multiplied by 52 and divided by 12 (4.33 weeks per month), while biweekly items use 26 pay periods divided by 12. This ensures your monthly numbers reflect true annual cash flows.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 6: Budget Planner Templates -->
    <article class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[#0f172a] dark:text-[#f8fafc]" data-mrb-t="home.ed_s6_title">
